window.__labLoaded({"id":"7.1","title":"Chi-Square Tests for Categories","goal":"Test whether ad type (video or banner) is related to buying, using the lesson's 200-person table, and measure the size of the link with Cramér's V.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the table of counts","text":"Rows are the two ads and columns are bought or not. A chi-square test works on counts like these, never on percentages. Totals first, so you can see where the expected counts will come from.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\n# rows: video ad, banner ad    columns: bought, did not buy\nobserved = np.array([[60, 40],\n                     [45, 55]])\n\nprint('row totals   :', observed.sum(axis=1))\nprint('column totals:', observed.sum(axis=0))\nprint('grand total  :', observed.sum())","out":"row totals   : [100 100]\ncolumn totals: [105  95]\ngrand total  : 200","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the chi-square test","text":"chi2_contingency works out the expected counts (what we would see if ad type and buying were unrelated), then the chi-square statistic, degrees of freedom and p-value. correction=False gives the plain formula used in the lesson.","code":"res = stats.chi2_contingency(observed, correction=False)\n\nprint('expected counts:')\nprint(res.expected_freq)\nprint(f'chi-square = {res.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df         = {res.dof}')\nprint(f'p-value    = {res.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"expected counts:\n[[52.5 47.5]\n [52.5 47.5]]\nchi-square = 4.511\ndf         = 1\np-value    = 0.0337","figs":[]},{"title":"Check it by hand","text":"Expected = row total x column total / grand total. Each cell then adds (observed - expected)^2 / expected, and the four pieces sum to the same chi-square.","code":"rows = observed.sum(axis=1)\ncols = observed.sum(axis=0)\nn = observed.sum()\n\nexpected = np.outer(rows, cols) / n\npieces = (observed - expected) ** 2 / expected\n\nprint(pieces.round(3))\nprint(f'sum of pieces = {pieces.sum():.3f}')","out":"[[1.071 1.184]\n [1.071 1.184]]\nsum of pieces = 4.511","figs":[]},{"title":"Measure the effect with Cramér's V","text":"A small p-value says the link is probably real, not how big it is. Cramér's V = sqrt(chi-square / (N x (k - 1))), where k is the smaller of the number of rows and columns. It runs from 0 (no link) to 1 (perfect link).","code":"chi2 = res.statistic\nk = min(observed.shape)\nv = np.sqrt(chi2 / (n * (k - 1)))\n\nprint(f'Cramer V = {v:.3f}')\nprint('bought: video', observed[0, 0] / rows[0], ' banner', observed[1, 0] / rows[1])","out":"Cramer V = 0.150\nbought: video 0.6  banner 0.45","figs":[]},{"title":"Bonus: goodness of fit","text":"A goodness-of-fit test checks one set of counts against a claimed pattern. Here a die was rolled 120 times; chisquare assumes every face is equally likely unless you give it other expected counts.","code":"rolls = np.array([25, 17, 15, 23, 24, 16])\n\nfit = stats.chisquare(rolls)\n\nprint(f'expected per face = {rolls.sum() / 6:.0f}')\nprint(f'chi-square = {fit.statistic:.3f}, df = {len(rolls) - 1}')\nprint(f'p-value    = {fit.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"expected per face = 20\nchi-square = 5.000, df = 5\np-value    = 0.4159","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"ads <- matrix(c(60, 45, 40, 55), nrow = 2,\n              dimnames = list(ad = c(\"video\", \"banner\"), bought = c(\"yes\", \"no\")))\ntest <- chisq.test(ads, correct = FALSE)\ntest\ntest$expected\nsqrt(test$statistic / (sum(ads) * (min(dim(ads)) - 1)))\nchisq.test(c(25, 17, 15, 23, 24, 16))","expect":"X-squared = 4.5113, df = 1, p-value = 0.03365; expected counts 52.5, 47.5, 52.5, 47.5; Cramer's V = 0.150; die: X-squared = 5, df = 5, p-value = 0.4159","note":"correct = FALSE turns off the Yates continuity correction that R applies to 2 by 2 tables by default, so the numbers match the lesson and the Python. R has no built-in Cramer's V, so the last line computes it by hand."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Crosstabs (Statistics: Chi-square, Phi and Cramer's V; Cells: Expected)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / ad buy count.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 1 60  1 2 40  2 1 45  2 2 55\nEND DATA.\nVALUE LABELS ad 1 'video' 2 'banner' / buy 1 'bought' 2 'did not buy'.\nWEIGHT BY count.\nCROSSTABS\n  /TABLES=ad BY buy\n  /STATISTICS=CHISQ PHI\n  /CELLS=COUNT EXPECTED.","note":"Read the Pearson Chi-Square row (SPSS also prints a continuity-corrected row for 2 by 2 tables, ignore it here). Cramer's V is in the Symmetric Measures table. WEIGHT BY count tells SPSS each row stands for that many people."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput ad buy freq\n1 1 60\n1 2 40\n2 1 45\n2 2 55\nend\ntabulate ad buy [fweight = freq], chi2 V expected","note":"tabulate with chi2 gives the Pearson chi-square, df and p-value; V adds Cramer's V; expected prints the expected counts. The goodness-of-fit bonus has no one-line built-in in Stata, so it is shown only in Python and R."},"try":["Change the banner row to 50 and 50 (so both ads convert 50 to 60 percent) and predict what happens to chi-square and Cramer's V.","Multiply every count by 2 (400 people, same percentages). Which changes, the p-value or Cramer's V, and why?"]});
